INT: Today is Sunday, June 14, 1998. I am conducting an interview with Edward Malinowski. My name is Jennifer Rube. We are in West Bloomfield, Michigan, in the United States. And the interview is being conducted in English.

Today is Sunday, June 14th. My name is Jennifer Rube. And I am conducting an interview with Dr. Edward Malinowski. We are in West Bloomfield, Michigan, in the United States. And the interview is being conducted in English.

What is your name?

EM: My name is Edward Malinowski, M-A-L-I-N-O-W-S-K-I.

INT: Was that your only name?

EM: No. My original name is-- was Edward Mersyk, M-E-R-S-Y-K.

INT: And when is your birth date?

EM: I was born in Warsaw, May 14, 1939.

INT: Who were your parents?

EM: My parents were Stefania Mersyk, maiden name, Frendzel, F-R-E-N-D-Z-E-L, and father, Marek, M-A-R-E-K. They were-- my mother was born in Warsaw, as well. My father was born in a city called Kovel, K-O-W-E-L, I think, at present spelled K-O-V-E-L, which was part of Poland. And right now, it's part of Ukraine.

Both my parents were lawyers before the war, were practicing lawyers. I know my father had-- was a partner. You know, he's got own office. My mother was already working on one of the big office in Warsaw. I knew that the main patron, I think, later on, was living in Australia, but I'm not sure about it.

I know even exactly place when I was born in Warsaw. I was born on a-- in a clinic, in a hospital, on the street of Wielki, W-I-E-L-K-I which translate to 'big.' That was one of the street in the middle of Warsaw. It doesn't exist anymore, because this part of Warsaw was destroyed in the war. And presently, in this area, there is a huge building, most prominent building in Warsaw built by Russians, so like smack center in Warsaw.

INT: Where was your family living?

EM: We are living in Warsaw on a street corner of Nowolipki, N-O-W-O-L-I-P-K-I, number 13, which is-- which is present-- not present, but it's completely different. This was, again, the center of Warsaw ghetto later on.

INT: Did you have brothers or sisters?

EM: No, I did not have any brothers or sisters.

INT: Did anyone else live in your house?

EM: During the ghetto time, yes, apparently, a lot of people lived at our house. And my first recollection, really, are from this house, from this apartment. I remember this apartment.

My first recollection-- I was a small child. I was three months old when the Second World War started-- was from this particular apartment. I remember standing on balcony watching some-- like there was a small like a park, like a garden in front of it. And I was watching it. But I knew it was a lot of people in this-- living in this-- this apartment.

Matter of fact, this apartment, right now, if I remember correctly, is almost like without walking. It's extremely close to monument of heroes of Warsaw ghetto. And of course, it doesn't exist, because after the ghetto, ghetto uprising, this part of Warsaw was completely destroyed and was rebuilt after the war in a different style.

That was my first recollection of-- from, from that time, from Warsaw ghetto. I remember a few other things. And some of them came out to me in like a repressed memory.

I remember, in like the mid-- late '50s or early '60s, I was watching some-- for the first time, some movie, documentary movie about the Warsaw ghetto and Warsaw during the war. I think it was-- movie was made by Swedes.

And I remember, specifically, that we were crossing. There was a-- like a wooden bridge between two parts of ghetto. There was a streetcar coming from a good part of Warsaw through this area, and to divide, to separate, not permit Jews to, to, to, to go on this area, there was a wall on both side.

There was like a wooden bridge. We were crossing this bridge, I remember, very often. That was like the middle of Warsaw, because, apparently, they couldn't move the streetcar line from, from that area.

We are staying, for sure, in this apartment with my maternal great grandfather-- grandfather. His name was Jakub. And that was-- I remember his silhouette. I don't remember his face. I remember his silhouette, because-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

And my, my parents were working in a shop, which is like a-- there was-- the German organized, in Warsaw ghetto, many places for Jews to work and to contribute to German war effort. And from, too, I know they were making like brushes and repairing old uniform.

And apparently, they left me with grandfather. And in ghetto, from time to time, they were like doing like a liquidation. They were picking up Jews and sending them to Treblinka. And my grandfather was hiding me in the loft during the one of these liquidation. And my parents were not aware about it that it was happening.

So I remember, we were founded there. Two, I think, German came. One was in a, a civil uniform, one was specifically was wearing a leather coat and one was a probably gendarme. I remember was a helmet.

And my father was playing-- praying at that time. He was-- he was praying. He was in tallit. And they came to him. And the German soldier, I remember, beat him up with the butt of rifle.

I remember it very vividly. It's, it's funny, because I was a small boy. One way, it was horrible for me, what he was doing. Second, and I remember specifically the rifle. I can even describe exactly how it looked like and so on. And from that place, we were taken to umschlagplatz. And we were ready to be deported to Treblinka.

But that time, my father founded that we were taken. And apparently, from what my mother told me, he bribed Jewish policemen. And he was able to get me out. He didn't have enough money to get my grandfather out.

I think he some way, he faked it that I had a-- some infectious disease, typhoid fever or whatever. And they took me to some small, like not hospital, but like a place where they keep some sick, sick people.

I remember, I was carrying out on a very, very steep stairways to get to that place. And, and that was the last time we heard about our grandfather, of course.

And then, from Warsaw ghetto, I remember I was smuggled to, to so-called good part of Warsaw, Aryan part of Warsaw. I remember, specifically, they put me in a big lorry. And I was very upset, because I was in the bottom of it. And I knew that. And on top of me, they put a lot of packages. And most of the packages, they had something to eat. And I knew there was mostly like marmalade, a jam made from beets. And I was very hungry. I want to eat it, but I couldn't, because everything was cover. Apparently, my mother, my father knew what's coming, and he was trying to get us some place in-- on the other side of Warsaw.

INT: When was this?

EM: This was probably March of 1943.

INT: Do you have other family at the time?

EM: Yes. I knew that that time in Warsaw ghetto was also my paternal parents. I don't remember them at all. And from--

INT: Do you know what happened to them?

EM: They died. But if they were kill or die from starvation, I have no idea. But I know they never ever left Warsaw ghetto. Because they died at that time when we were still there. Also, at that time, in the Warsaw ghetto, with us were my mother's sister and her daughter. A matter of fact, from that moment on, our whole story to-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] during the time or war was very, very close. We was all the time together.

INT: What were their names?

EM: Name is-- presently, her name is Dr. Kristina Kohanowska. I think she's doing also an interview. Her original name was Kohn, K-O-H-N. And her mother's name was Janina. And there were two sister. My, my mother-- my mother had one sister. There was another sister.

My father did have some family, I think. I know for sure he had a brother who emigrated from Poland long before the war. And he, matter of fact, immigrated to United States. But we have no contact with him, at that time, I know for sure. There was also, apparently, some cousins who emigrated in 20-- in '20s to, to, at that time, Palestine. They were very much involved in the Zionist movement. And they moved there. But, again, I have no, no knowledge about it.

So after we left Warsaw ghetto, which was about a month-- a month before Warsaw uprising, we were staying in a few places in Warsaw. But then we stayed, most of the time, in Warsaw on a street called Grójecka, G-R-O-J-E-C-K-I. I remember the street number, I think, was 101.

Why we stay in this place? Warsaw was bombarded and partially damaged during the beginning of the Second World War in September. And damage at that time was not severe, but several houses were damaged.

Apparently, my father got in touch with some Polish family, who was living in this apartment building. And he simply paid them to rebuild. Part of their apartment was damaged during this bombardment in 1939. And because of this, they let us come to this apartment. So we were hiding in this.

INT: Do you know whose apartment it was?

EM: Their name was Smykowski, S-M-Y-K-O-W-S-K-I.

INT: Was your father hiding with you at this time?

EM: Initially, my father was hiding with me. So initially, in the apartment were me, my mother, and my father. As you can see, now, and my mother was the same, we had so-called good look. In other word, I was the one who totally could go on the street, and people not recognize me as a Jewish person.

My father had the, unfortunately, bad look. And they were very nervous about him be-- staying in the apartment. The people recognize him. And they giving him trouble and so on.

So apparently, after a few weeks in the apartment, my father also wanted to, to not stay there. But he wanted to join underground. And they told him they're going to get in touch with underground to get him to underground Polish movement.

And a few weeks later, indeed, two people happened to show up in our apartment. I remember, vaguely, that they came. And my father went with them. A few days later, my mother found out that they took him directly to Gestapo in Warsaw and he was killed. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

I don't know, exactly, if this is exact, whether it was Warsaw ghetto or he was taken someplace else. But apparently, they got some knowledge that this happened in Warsaw. And that, that's the last thing that I know about my father.

INT: Have you tried to find out details?

EM: I tried to find out. There was no way to find out. The war-- after the war, we couldn't find any more details. Matter of fact, even lately, I was contacting through American Red Cross, the International Red Cross, trying to find out, and they search on, on my father. And there is no, no-- not any information available.

That's, that's it about my father. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And my father, apparently, was a very knowledgeable person. And of course, everybody would say about his father. But he was very smart. He was also got many hobby. One of them was he was an expert in an international language called Esperanto. He was corresponding many people around the world, apparently, this and so on

I got only one picture of my father. I remember him, vaguely. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] Of course, after this, my mother was very devastated.

And my--her sister and her daughter, also, at that time escaped from Warsaw ghetto, because it was liquidation, all at the same time, in April. And they, they were hiding many place. And around Warsaw, they couldn't find a place to hide.

So my mother went to owner of the apartments said, they want them to come to our place. And she said, no, because my cousin, that's from my aunt's daughter, got Jewish look. And she's going to cause trouble, again.

My mother was so desperate, told them if she's not going to take them, she's going on the street and tell people that she's hiding Jews. [CRYING] She has no reason for living. Her father-- her father die-- her, her husband died. We are going to die. So what's the difference?

So finally she agree. And we, we were hiding-- in other words, accepted.

INT: Did you have false papers?

EM: We got false paper all the time. We're changing name all the time. However, I was not confined to this apartment. We couldn't be confined, because we have to live. And my mother and her sister were trying everything possible to support us.

And I knew they were mostly doing like buying some stuff, mostly some, some cookies, some cakes and selling them, always traveling, selling, trying to make. But every time they go in the street, they were very nervous on whether they're going to catch them.

Matter of fact, once on the street, somebody recognized my mother. And they took a coat from her. They, they told her that, give me the coat, or we send you-- we give you to the Germans. But they were supporting us, some degree.

But I remember it were a very difficult time. Not so-- we never see a sugar, butter during the war. [LAUGHS] Not because we weren't supposed to be there, but simply there was nothing available. And I was standing-- sitting, most of the time, in the apartment. My cousin was staying all the time in the apartment.

And when my mother was taking me someplace, because she has to take me, I always was nervous. Because I did not know what's my name for this week. We're changing my name. Many, many times, my name was Malinowski. But I remember, we change also name, one, one which I remember is [? Fabiswewski, ?] F-A-B-I-S-Z-E-W-S-I.

So people always apparently saying, such a nice boy. He knows he is such smart, but he doesn't know his last name. I did not know what is name for this week.

Also, I was very much embarrassment to my mother. An embarrassment, because my mother was very nervous, because we are-- apparently we were traveling in a area around the Warsaw, on a-- on a electric train.

There was the special train in Warsaw called EKD, EKD, which is like a train, going from central Warsaw to some small places around the Warsaw. And every time a German would come in, going, going through all the papers, whatever, I was hiding under the bench. I was afraid of Germans so much at that time. So, they were very nervous I would cause them potential problem.

I was-- I remember this apartment, very well and staying in the apartment very well. Matter of fact, I was spending a lot of time watching through the window. And I think I witnessed, also, a arrest of-- there was a person who wrote the DiaryofWarsawGhetto.I think his name is [? Rosenblum. ?]

And I remember, specifically, when the German came. And again, because I was a boy, I was fascinated. There came like a heavy truck, which was like a special truck, which got truck in the back and the wheel in front. And they arrest a lot of people, who were treated from a apartment building across our street, down the street, called Opaczewska.

So that was apparently his arrest, which I noted. And we were in this place, as I say, till beginning of the Warsaw uprising. And again, so it was my mother, her sister and her daughter-- her daughter and me.

INT: When was this this?

EM: This was between 1943 and 1944. Warsaw uprising started in August of 1944. And happens, while locked up there, this place was also very much involved in Warsaw uprising. And we were hiding with other occupants of this apartment building in a basement, because was a lot of shooting going on and so on.

This part of Warsaw, at that time, was almost outskirt of Warsaw, in a part of Warsaw called Ochota, O-C-H-O-T-A. And not far from this place is the main airport of Warsaw, which was, during the war, airport used also for military reason, as well. Why I'm saying this? Because that is quite an interesting aspect of the history.

Anyway, when we were in this building, one time in the basement we were there. Matter of fact, I remember, [NON-ENGLISH] was very happy, because this caught my mother and her sister a moment when she was supposed to deliver some, some stuff to some other places, so there was something to eat. Because they were not able to deliver, we are able to eat this.

And there was a rumor that the Germans are coming to this basement. So people start running. And I remember this group split. One of them, which was on our group, went outside of this building to like a-- because on outskirt, there were vegetable gardens. I mean, people cultivating some vegetables, some fruits to, to be able to survive. And the other group went a different direction. Turn out, we found out, a few days later, the group was killed on the spot, immediately, in this apartment building.

And we were-- we were hiding. There was a vegetable garden or bushes. I remember, specifically, we were hiding in raspberry bushes. We spent there about four or five days.

And the-- unfortunate, the weather was very nice. It was very hot. And we were extremely-- I remember, I was extremely thirsty. We were eating only this raspberry, whatever it was around us. This was-- I think it was the end of August of 1944.

One of the day, we were discovered by a German soldier. And while I was saying by the airport, because they was probably solely from Luftwaffe. Because I remember they wearing different uniform, like a gray-blue whatever.

And when he founded us, he apparently went up to [INAUDIBLE]. And he, he give us. At that time, he gave us some, I remember, it was like a bag of carrot. And he say, he will come later on, bring some more food, and will try to get us out of this. He was trying to help us, which was somewhat unusual.

Unfortunately, a few hours later, to the same place, came some soldier, which was Ukrainian or Russian. And he immediately are screaming that there are people here. Matter of fact, he look on my cousins, say, Juden, that they are Jewish.

And he didn't have even a rifle. I remember, specific, he had in his hand, hand grenade. I remember, it was hand grenade, wooden, wooden handle. And he start pushing us. And we were passing through like a broken fence. And we got in the front of this building facing street Opaczewska.

What's happened? At that time, I think German used to [INAUDIBLE] Warsaw uprising, they used also between others. So there is something called Kaminski Brigade, that I later found out was like a part of a army of renegade of Russian, mostly Russian soldier, who were involved in this. And their headquarter was exact in this building.

So they put up in the front of this. And apparently was general sitting there. I remember, specifically, he got like the [? Victrola ?] next to him. He was listening to some records or whatever.

And the soldier said that he founded them. My mother, especially my aunt, knew German very well, so they knew exactly what's going on. And they said they are Juden, we kill them. And they were ready to put us in front of a-- the building to shoot us.

And then my-- and then my mother start crying, that they are not-- we are not Juden. And we know how to pray. And my cousins start praying.

And then the German soldier was standing on the side, I would say, 30, 40 meters. They were on the side of the building. There were a group of them. I don't know how many, 10, 15, stop-- screaming, stop killing. No more killing. That's enough. And they not permitted this, Ukrainian and Russian kill us.

They took us across the street. And across the street, there was a market called [? Jelenia. ?] [? Jelenia ?] means like a-- [? zielony ?] means green, that's selling all of the greens before the war. And this was a place when they kept Poles, Warsaw citizens who were evacuated. They throw them out of apartment and whatever, and they were evacuating them out of Warsaw. They were sending them to camps or whatever.

So we are placed in this place. I remember that my cousin promptly collapsed there, because she was dehydrated. They give us some water. It was horrible water but at least it was some water. We stayed there not long.

I think we stayed there less than a day. And then they walk us to some other place. And finally, we end up, I think, the same day or next day in a place in Pruszków, P-R-U-S-Z-K-O-W, which was like a transit camp for citizens of Warsaw whom they kick out of Warsaw during the Warsaw uprising. And they were sending them out.

In this place, we stay, probably, again, no more than one day. And they escape. This was not like a camp. It was like a transit place. My mother and her sister took us to other places. And then we were hiding.

INT: How did you escape?

EM: This was not well controlled, I think, from what I remember. This was simply like that, they were keeping people there to get-- put them on a train. And so it was not like a control, like Warsaw ghetto, nothing like this. It was-- they walk out. I think that's what they did.

Because they knew this area relatively well, because that was the area when they were, before using, when they were selling stuff and so on, trying to survive. They took us, initially, more specifically to a place called Grodzisk, G-R-O-D-Z-I-S-K, which is not far. Everything is a matter of few kilometers one place to another.

And at this place, I remember we stayed. Well, this apartment was horrible, because there was rats running all over us. It was a terrible place. We stayed there a very short time, like a week or two weeks. Then we went to some farm. We stay in a farm for two, three weeks.

We have to move again, because, again, they were afraid of hiding Jews there. And then we end up in a place called-- finally, between other places. I think we stayed one other place, but, finally, we went to a place called Zyrardów. It is not exactly the spelling, Z-Y-R-A-R-D-O-W.

This is a small but industrial city not far from Warsaw. It's about 30 kilometers south, I would say, of Warsaw. Due to some irony, before the Second World War, my parents, besides being lawyers, also, my mother, when they finally start making some money, invested some money.

And they build a small factory in Warsaw, which was making stockings. And they imported some super modern machinery. Matter of fact, they got some supervisor from Germany to help to organize. Well, later on, found out that immediately after German occupy Warsaw, they took the machine into this place, Zyrardów, which apparently stayed for many, many years thereafter.

Anyway, in the Zyrardów, I think we didn't stay in the same apartment. My-- we stay in different apartment. And my sister stay in different apartment. I, I remember I was staying-- most of the time, my mother was leaving me alone. There was no babysitter at that time. And I was about four years old.

And I was extremely afraid of German. But the same thing, I remember, I was fascinating. I remember from my window, I was, through the very small thing, I was looking at them. Because I've seen them marching in-- three in a row and singing, [SINGING IN GERMAN]. And I was very-- how, how to put it? I didn't have any toys, nothing. For just me it was, was, fascinating, just how they looked like.

Especially, I was afraid and also fascinated of something called [NON-ENGLISH]. [NON-ENGLISH], that was, I think, a German gendarmerie, military police. They were wearing like a big thing, like in front of them, with the name of the, the department that was. The most of them, they were always in the helmets. And they looked very, very Teutonic, let's put it this way.

And that would be staying in this Zyrardów, my mother, at that time, also with her sister trying to support us. They were-- what they were doing? From what I know, they were buying stolen cotton from this textile factory, which Polish worker were smuggling out of factory.

And then they were selling them, selling them around the Warsaw. But also, all the way down to place called Czestochowa. So they have to travel a lot. And they got a lot of trouble, because, a few times, they were stopped and so on for two reason.

Number one, this was very illegal, because it was the German war effort. And the second time, a few times, they recognized they were Jewish. But luckily from them, they were able to, to get out of there.

A matter of fact, I was staying Zyrardów. My cousin was going with them. So she probably give you more in details about the, the story in her interview. I remember on many occasion, we were very hungry, because there was nothing to eat. The same situation, what they were doing, that was-- [INAUDIBLE] simply to, to somebody survive.

Finally, one of the day, it was in January of '40-- '45, we were liberated by Russian. I remember a Russian tank came in the, the middle of the street. It was a very cold winter. And a Russian soldier came out of this. I remember it was very cold. They give me a big glove. I put two of my hand in one glove. And that was liberation.

And a few days after, it was on-- and Warsaw was liberated on the January 17. My mother and her sister went to Warsaw two days later. And one of the first people came to Warsaw, and they walk, which is about 30 kilometers in wintertime. And winter were quite severe. Then they came back to, to Zyrardów to pick us up.

INT: Did you stay alone when they left?

EM: I stay alone. I think I-- I'm not even sure that I stayed with my cousin that time. Because remember, we were in different apartments hiding. And-- but that time, not hiding anymore. And then they, they came to pick us up. And we went back to Warsaw.

So we came to Warsaw. We were in Warsaw a few days after liberation.

INT: Did you also walk?

EM: From there, I remember, yes. We have to walk this. Because there wasn't nothing available. And Warsaw was completely destroyed in the war, like 95% or more Warsaw was destroyed. Of course, our house was destroyed, everything else was destroyed.

However, surprisingly, their house, which was on [NON-ENGLISH] street, was not destroyed. It survived the war. And they moved to that. We moved to the same house. And, and I remember, we live in one of the apartment. Of course, there was no heating. And-- but then we put like a small stove in the middle of the apartment.

And on top of this, because when the building was partially demolished, they were recommend dangerous. At that time, military was destroying other buildings, so were exploding all the time. Any time we get exploding, our whatever window we had left were-- window, glasses were breaking. So I remember, our supervisor was coming, putting some like-- not even plywood, because plywood not available. Something else to, to, to, to, to, to keep us some way warm, which was very, very difficult.

INT: Did you have any trouble? Did your aunt have any trouble getting her apartment back?

EM: Apartment? Yes. And now that there were-- there, there were, yes, but in a general aspect of this. Because they own this house, before the war, they were owners of this house, they got special apartment, I remember, on the fourth floor.

When they came for the first time to this apartment, was a few days after liberation of Warsaw. They found that somebody who was looting this apartment. And they have to chase him out of this apartment.

Then we lived whatever apartment floor was available, not completely just damaged, because it was a lot of rubble, whatever. Because this house was one of the very few left in Warsaw, when Polish government moved to Warsaw, they began looking for a place when to put-- for an institution.

And they decided then our-- this building, they put something called Film Polski, which is like a main, government-controlled Polish cinematography. And then they passed some law, which everything was nationalized.

So the building was taken from the family. And we were told to move out of the building to a-- several we could use-- negotiate-- they were able-- we were able to move to one other apartment.

Finally, finally, they move us to, to one of the apartment, top floor, because they occupy first five floors of the building. And matter of fact, we stay in this apartment for many, many years. It was on sixth floor on street Marszalkowska, M-A-R-S-Z-A-L-K-O-W-S-K-A, 56. And this, this building exists till today.INT: --school when you went back to Warsaw?

EM: We, as I say, we were in this building. And it was the middle of winter. It was January. It was very, very bad winter. At that time, too, was no heating, no transportation, still were full of rubbles. There was no, there was no way even to work.

There was no communication and so on. And-- but people had to live. And my mother and her sister were very resilient, the same as my father who really, due to his some way prediction of what's going to happen, was able to get us to this apartment during the war.

They started looking for, possibly to working. And both of them very, very shortly began to work. Both of them were lawyers by profession. And you have to know a little bit more about the situation in Poland in the '40s and '50s.

Almost everybody worked [? wherever. ?] There was no private enterprise, practically none. My mother began to work as a clerk in the Department of Health, like a legal clerk or whatever. You could call it legal advisor.

And they have to go to work to support us. And they have to do something with us. And so my cousin went to school. I was at that time five years old. According to Polish regulation, you go to elementary school at that time when you were seven years old.

So was two years ahead of time, but I'd say there was today, was, from that time, my cousin-- she couldn't stand that I was staying home and she was going to school-- so she enrolled me to school. They-- I think they falsified my papers, saying I was two years older. And--

INT: What name were you using at this time?

EM: Malinowski at that time. Matter of fact, at that time was different. Some day was using Mersyk and some days Malinowski, because that was last name when we were having.

And my mother, I think, for a long time couldn't decide which one to stay with, because some of the papers which she had-- matter of fact, there is one of them showing still name Malinowski-- Mersyk.

But I think starting, like, '47 we were going to use Malinowski most of the time. We went to school. I went to school. And this school by that time, I remember, specifically, was in some building which never was meant to be for school.

And they got, like, three classes. One class-- one row was first class, one was second, one, third one. And they were not teaching us much because there was no teachers. And but simply it was to keep me for a few hours at least in, in-- outside of a house with somebody watching me.

And this was School Number, I think, 40. And then they moved the school to-- there was an [NON-ENGLISH], and then they moved the school to [NON-ENGLISH].

But teaching was so poor, and I was so young. And there was so much-- how to put it-- so, so much distress from war and everything else, I was not learning anything.

Additionally, I was-- which it's difficult to-- to imagine, looking at me, now-- I was malnourished after the war. I was very skinny, and I was in horrible physical shape. And I remember I was twice in the special camp to be, to be fed, where they did a pretty good job, as you can see right now.

And I got some pictures showing me, that I was a skinny person [LAUGHS] some time ago. First camp which I went to was unusual one was a place called [NON-ENGLISH]. And it was like a camp.

I don't know exactly who organized. I think it was organized by a Joint, because it was only Jewish kids there who survived the ghettos or other places. And they were trying to take care of us. That was the summertime.

And these people now, they're all around the world. But one of the person who my cousin knows-- and I know him very well-- is still in Warsaw. He's a professor of medicine and [? Chief One ?] with the department. His name is Schneiderman. That was one camp.

Second time I was sent to camp, that was south Poland also to, to be fed and to-- being of-- physically and so on. I remember also that the representative of the International Red Cross, mostly from Denver, were coming, was giving us some other food supplements and so on.

And finally, which was for us the biggest feast, was UNRRA began to send in some parcels. And finally-- UNRRA was United Nations Relief Organization, I think, with most of them from this country, coming food.

Strange one, as it sound like for us, but it was something to eat. And every time we were looking for those UNRRA packages coming to finally be able to eat something.

And so we moved to the sixth floor of this apartment. And we stayed there till-- I-- I stayed there at least till I got married.

INT: Had your-- did you and your mother consider leaving Poland?

EM: I think first priority for them was, after they survive war to, to, to enjoy, first of all, that we are finally-- we don't have to be worried about going on the street-- be catch and kill and whatever. So at, at least, we were happy that we are able to, to live.

And there was a question of survival, how to, how to survive. They didn't have to work and so on. They got few friends who survived the war from our family. From my family there was-- from my direct family, as I say, my grandparents from paternal side died during the war.

And there was nobody else left. My aunt had two brother-in-law, who were someplace to work, but I don't think at that time we even knew where, where they are.

There were after, I think, some way settling for about a year or two, they began to think about maybe emigrating from Poland. Some way, another, my mother found out that my father brother was living at that time, United States.

I think he was living Elizabeth, New Jersey, from what I know. Again, from this, what my mother told me, he was not well-to-do man. He was not a rich man at all, but still he apparently wanted very much us to come to this country.

And he, I remember, he sent us a few food packages. And then he sent some affidavit to get the visa. And my mother was proceeding this to consulate immigration. Unfortunately, he died at that time when that was sent.

Then they were not-- that would be very difficult for them to immigrate, to figure that out. With their professions, they were not involved in any manual labor or whatever. There was not-- it would be very difficult for them to, to emigrate, with being lawyers and so on.

And they were, they were happy with this, being able to live there. And as many-- our new friends were decided, we decided to stay there. So I was going to school. I was going to, as I said, elementary school.

I also was very much enjoying music, and I start playing violin. And as most of the Jewish mother wanted, they wanted me to be violin wunderkind, so I was playing violin very seriously.

I got some picture of this. My mother wanted me to be a violinist for quite a while. And I was going to elementary school, and I was also going to a school of violin which was a little bit different than this country, because of, again, government sponsors.

So I have to take some theory. It was like a serious teaching of violin, preparing for profession being a teacher or a professional musician. After the elementary school, I went to a-- my high school was a-- high school, at that time, was Number 2, something called [? TPD. ?] [? TPD ?] stands for [NON-ENGLISH], which translates, like, the Society of Friends of Children with organized school which were like-- how to put it-- not religious school.

In the '40s, I think, some of the school in Poland required prayer and so on. This was completely not involved in religion, almost like here. There was no course, nothing in the school.

Matter of fact, in my class, which I was in for many years, I would say half of the kids were Jewish. Why they were Jewish? Very few, I think, from what I can see now-- talking to one of my friend who lived in this country-- I'm trying to remember if any of them were from Warsaw originally.

Most of them, not. Most of them were children of Jews from east part of Poland who, during the war with Russia-- and with our Polish Army, Russian-- and Polish Army came to Poland.

And the parents were working government and so on, so they were in this, this class. Matter of fact, right now all of them around the world, including Israel, in this country, and a few of them, Australia.

I was in this school till 1955. In 1955, I graduated from the school. And I was still playing violin. I also went to Warsaw University and studied musicology for two years.

Then I decided to switch. And I-- when we went before, but I was very much interested in medicine. I went to medical school. When I was in medical school, I finished my, like, [? middle ?] education for violin.

And I took exam to, like, a university school in music. But then they give me choice-- I have to decide if I'm going with medicine or in School of music. At that time, I decided that I could be a good physician, and it's better be good physician than good musician.

I couldn't imagine myself playing all the time in the Warsaw Opera, the second violin, something which I don't like it. So that would then decided. So I decided to stay in with medicine.

INT: Had-- at this point had you experienced, kind of in your later school years, any antisemitism?

EM: No.

INT: Anything left over from the war?

EM: Not really. Not in this time. Many of my friends, I would say I didn't-- I-- how to put it? I didn't manifest myself that I am Jewish. I was not practicing Jewish as 99.99% Jews in Warsaw.

I knew only one Jew at school, religious. Matter of fact, I am-- admire him very much now, because he was on a quite a high position in government. But he always was going to synagogue. Matter of fact, he took me once to synagogue.

My, my direct parents, my-- that means my mother and-- not parents-- my father and my aunt were not religious. But they always knew when the high holidays are coming, because he was telling them, and so on.

And they were-- how to put it-- maybe not celebrating, but they knew. And this was something like in the inside of the spirit. For some, my-- apparently, my grandparents from my father's sides were Hasidim.

And as I say, my, my grandpa-- and my grandpa was killed at that time. It was when, practically when he was playing. But people who knew that I am Jewish, I have no problem whatsoever.

Again, I was maybe a little bit unique situation, because I was in Warsaw. I was in some-- I wouldn't say an intellectual one, was-- we were educated, and this was really not a problem, despite the fact that there was a reason for some of the problems.

And this is something which is very rarely discussed in this country-- about some of Jews who came back to Poland from Russia, especially, and they were extremely, extremely involved in the Communist movement in, in all of those countries.

Half of them, close to half of the members of Politburo of Polish Communist Party were the unfortunately Jews. But that's a different story. This was- that was life till the time immigrated.

Of course, by meantime something very important happened. I finished my medical school. After I finished medical school, I was one of the best students in the school. So I was-- even-- even in the time of the schooling, I was awarded special, so-called scientific scholarship.

And then I was given a special type of scholarship, which was-- you can call, like, a graduate assistant-ship. I was taking care of patient in, in a university hospital in Warsaw. I was also involved in teaching students and doing research.

I got married, my wife, Jolanta in 1964.

INT: What was her maiden name?

EM: Her name maiden was Okuniewicz, O-K-U-N-I-E-W-I-C-Z. She's--

INT: And how did she spell the first name?

EM: Jolanta, J-O-L-A-N-T-A. She's not Jewish. She is-- she was born in a city called Cmielow, C-M-I-E-L-O-W, which is a small city not far from Radom, R-A-D-O-M.

The city is famous from two thing that's famous. One thing is known that it's one of the most prominent-- one of the best Polish china comes from the city. Everybody worked for this company till today. And also, it was a huge Jewish community.

Matter of fact, she's always telling me that her mother was going down the street, the Jews were asking her-- because was very friendly with them. And her name, maiden name was [? Mosel, ?] so they always believed that her-- she must be Jewish, because of that maiden name, [? Mosel. ?]

But that was-- she was, she was from that place. And she was physician. She-- my, my wife is a physician. We were classmate. And we got married in 1964.

Later on the same year my daughter was born. Her name is Susan, Susan [? Marta. ?] Polish name is Zuzanna, Z-U-Z-A-N-N-A. We lived in a part of Warsaw called Saska Kepa. We moved, of course, at that apartment.

And we got a very small apartment, but we were quite happy. As it were, we were struggling as everybody else at that time. I work in the two, three places, trying to, to, to, to makes end meet-- my wife, also, the same situation.

We have to hire somebody to take care of our daughter. And-- but on a level which was at that time average, maybe slightly more average than that person, there we were quite happy. We were able to get a vacation-- maybe not a foreign country, but at least maybe to-- just two, three weeks vacation, to afford minor luxury of everyday life, and there were quite happy.

Then came famous 1968. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] What's happened 1968? This is again up to historian, and I can only tell you my perspective. Matter of fact, there was a lot of article written about it.

Polish Communist government began to be big trouble, of economically big trouble. And they did not know what-- how to handle the situation. And they found that-- and also was a big fight on between the fraction of the people in the Communist Party.

Some of them was very nationalistic. Anyway, they used this-- they used Jews as a pretext to kick out certain other people from top of the Communist Party.

And they explained that everything in Poland is so horrible because we still got Jews who have no alliance to Polish nation, to Polish government. Most are interested about the interests of Jews and Israel and in all of the old story.

They played the propaganda very, very well. And there was a very cruel propaganda. It was very unusual, because for a new generation-- it was completely new-- they did not know what they're talking about it.

But for older generations, remind them something which was going on before. And, and this was relatively easy to play, because it's true that quite a few Jews were still on a quite a high, prominent position.

This movement started because at the same time in Warsaw University and some other universities, there began to be movement against Polish Government, against Communism. Matter of fact, some of the prominent members of the movement were, again, Jewish people, young Jews who are still right now even were in the present government of Poland.

I was always very apolitical. And I was not participating in any organization. I was not participating in this socialism organization, communist, as well as Jewish organization.

There were some Jewish organizations-- not many in the Warsaw after the Second World War. There was some Jewish cultural life. As was well known, there was a Jewish theater with Ida Kaminska. There was a Jewish Historical Institute.

There was one synagogue, but there was very few people going to it at one-- but I was-- and the same thing, I was not involved in any, any, any other organization. I was doing my job as a physician. That practice I was doing.

Still at that moment, the Polish government decided that Jews are trouble for Poland, so we have to kick them out of Poland. And I was told in my medical school that I cannot be trusted as a-- not only as a physician, but mainly as a teacher to student-- that I am not politically correct for them.

And I have to leave this, so I was forced to, to resign from this position. My wife was still working for a while. And then they told her the same thing, because my wife was a-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] you know, I am a Jewish person, that this is our place, that Poland.

We were given something called travel document, not passport, because they strip us of Polish citizenship. And then something very illegal, because at that time, Poles signed some apparently organization-- United Nations organization, saying that they are not permitted to issue something like this.

And this document was saying, this is a document, travel document, saying that person presenting is not a Polish citizen. So I was stateless immediately. We are permitted to took very few belongings from Poland.

For example, many, many of my papers were not-- I'm not permitted to take it-- only my diploma, lucky for me, and some birth certificate-- very few pictures, which I don't have many, but very few pictures.

And we have to leave Poland. We did not know at that time when we are going to immigrate. We knew that we are going to Vienna, which was the first big West European city, passing Polish border.

And I remember we got a lot of harassment at that time. It's a complete different story. Like, we are passing in the middle of winter through the Polish border. We got-- all of our belongings were like, I think, 10 suitcases.

We didn't-- because we have to get everything-- your winter clothes and linens and towels and everything, whatever you can carry. And we were told that this is too much, so they are not going to check on this-- we have to go back to Warsaw.

But we knew already that if we bribed them, they will let us go. And that's what's happened. We arrived to Vienna. And we went to-- theoretically, we are immigrating to Israel.

I have however mixed feelings about immigrating to Israel, mainly, because my wife. I didn't want to start again a situation when I am going to be OK, and my wife is not going to be OK.

And I did not know if this is going to be true or not, but I didn't want to take my chances. And so I went to Jewish agency, and they release us. And I have to say they behaved very-- how to put it-- maybe noble is not correct story, but very understanding.

And they have no, no pressure whatsoever. Then I was told that there is a Joint and HIAS who takes care of Jewish refugees. So we went to these organizations.

They placed us-- one was a small pensione in Vienna. After one week, they move us to Italy, because there's too many refugees in Vienna at that time.There was a lot of refugees from Poland and from Czechoslovakia. That was after Czechoslovakian uprising. We spent about six months in Italy, waiting for a visa to immigrate.

INT: Was it just you and your wife and daughter?

EM: At that time, it was me, my wife, my daughter, and my mother. My mother was-- at that time, was in her 60s. And she was not a healthy person. We stayed in one room, of course. But I was extremely grateful that the Joint was helping us.

They were giving us a minimal amount of money, which was for us-- was enough, let's put it this way, for a subsistence like here-- you know, food stamps or maybe even worse. But we were able to, to live there.

And we waited mainly to, to get permission to immigrate, to organize immigration papers and so on. We could immigrate very easy with no difficulties whatsoever to a country like Sweden, Denmark, South Africa, Australia.

There were needed physicians. They wanted us immediately. There was government sponsored immigration. In other words, they will pay us for school. and so on. However, I felt that in Sweden and Denmark, my daughter never is going to be, you know, Hans, you know, Andersen.

She's always going to be foreign, and the same story, other place. If she has to start from beginning, you have to start beginning, the only country for us is United States. And that's what we waited for.

We were also in minority, because for most of the Poles and for most of the even Polish Jews, always kind of got some families here. We have none-- zero, zero, so.

And also there was some unusual situation, because for most of the Polish Jews, United States was New York and then New York only. And because I got sick and tired of the mess, I want to immigrate every place but New York. [LAUGHS]

So one of the day, social worker from HIAS came to us and asked, do we like to immigrate to Detroit? I was pretty good in geography, but I say, can you show me again where is Detroit?

Then she show us Detroit. Then she show us some-- went, I remember we went to, to American Embassy to look at some picture. Look on the picture-- it's very nice.

So I say, fine. And I say, what about working? And I did not know English at that time at all. And they say, no problem, there is a hospital in Detroit want you to work there, and they will take care of you and so on.

OK, fine. So after about six month, my cousins and her mother immigrated first. They came to this city. And we came here about a few months-- about a month, one month later.

Reality was a little bit different. And it was quite difficult in the beginning. We were placed in apartment on Ohio Street in Grand River, if you know this area-- where it is.

And it was one year after riots in Detroit. So I think it was only different color faces on the street, but we did not know about it. The second day somebody threw a window-- a stone in our window, so it was very, very, very difficult.

But after a while, we made it. I was-- for me, whole immigration was extremely traumatic. Why was traumatic? Not because I was immigrating from Poland, which I had thought my country. And till today, I believe that the country I was born, I was raised, I went to school-- that's the language I speak at home, and I was educated.

But at the same time, I-- when we immigrated, my wife left all of her family-- her parents, her sisters. We never knew when we are going to see them again. And in fact, it took us about almost 15 years before she was able to see her mother again. She came here, and, and it was very difficult.

INT: Have you been back to Poland?

EM: Yes, many, many times. Since then I was-- but initially, we couldn't go there, either because of, again, political situation. We are political refugee. We came here as a political refugee.

And my daughter was the first one who went there when she was about 12 or 13 years old. So it was very difficult to prove to Polish government that she is enemy of the people in the revolution in Poland, being 12 years old.

So they, they couldn't-- they could not give her not visa, so she, she went first. But then they will not let us to go to Poland, because they did not want to show people Poland that people who are kicked out of Poland-- terrible situation-- made it in in this country, whatever means made it.

But they permitted-- finally, finally, they permitted her mother, when her mother retired, to come here, which was about 15 years after, after we immigrated. So there I felt very much obligated to, to make the life easy for them as much as possible.

And it was not easy. We stay in a-- this place off Park. For us, of course, there was not no work, because we have to pass all of the exams and everything else. Lucky for me I was able to pass my ECFMG, which is like a special examination for physician on first approach. And I start working. And so it was that--

INT: How did you learn English?

EM: I learned a little bit in Rome. They organized school for us, which was organized by ORT, by the way, when we were staying in Rome. And then, and then in this country, I went for a short time to International Institute.

And I'm-- you have to, you have to. There was not a choice. And then I start working. And in Rome there was-- some people say, oh, you spent six months, Rome is wonderful. So wonderful we couldn't afford to buy a bus ticket.

So for example, I have to go to work, I have to walk, I remember, all [NON-ENGLISH] through [? Tiber. ?] In the hot weather some days I would rather have [INAUDIBLE] hours.

But it's true, after six months, I was better in Rome than, than the guide of Rome. I knew every possible place around then. But that's-- [LAUGHS] that was Rome. It was not a Roman vacation, especially when the-- not knowing where I'm going to be a few months from now, and you have no idea what your future is going to be and so on.

And then I start working at Sinai. And I think I was the only resident in the history of Sinai who was coming to Sinai-- for about six or seven months I couldn't afford to buy anything, because we were making-- outside, I had to support the whole family.

I was coming by bus. I was taking Schaefer bus to Outer Drive and then walking. Some days I was going by Porsche. I got friend of mine who was very rich, got Porsche, so occasionally was coming to my house, picking me up-- [LAUGHS] going by Porsche.

So that was a story. But then I was able to graduate. My wife was able also pass fine in the exam. And she started working. And she-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] again, it was unusual story.

She wanted always to be dermatologist. And in Poland, it was very difficult to get your dermatology as a matter of fact, because it was very-- looked at with specialty. One was the person who was given the most, the hardest time was there one of the Jewish person whom I knew.

Here she went to this interview. Matter of fact, American doctor says, why are you even going to review-- there is no, no, no, no, no reason for you to go there, you'll never get dermatology position.

But happens, that person who was interviewing who a famous dermatologist named Pinkus-- was a German Jewish-- Jewish, but from Germany. Anyway, he some way reminded himself his own story when he was starting in the beginning.

And they took her the program, and she finished as the best resident in the program, so I was very pleased with this. My daughter was doing very well in school, and we are living in Oak Park. Then we moved to Farmington, here.

She was always in the top of her class. She finished Andover High School, here. And I'm saying this because that's, that's what I'm most proud here-- that-- not what I did.

She, she finished as valedictorian, with honor everything, including English. However, I was always in trouble when I was going to school. And they look at me-- me is talking English. They say, she's got honor in English. [LAUGHS] It's-- can't be your daughter.

But anyway, she didn't go to bilingual education, and she made it. And after she finished Andover, she was accepted practically every place, including Harvard and Amherst College and Duke and Dartmouth.

And finally, she end up going to Inteflex Program in Medicine in the University of Michigan. And, and, and a matter of fact, they call her several times from Harvard-- how could she reject to be admitted with them-- they wanted to, to meet her.

Matter of fact, there was something which later on she told us-- and I still do not know details-- one of the reasons why they took her, beside of her grades and everything else, she happened to wrote essays, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] which some way described our struggle to get here and how she felt about the whole story about the war.

She finished her Inteflex then she went to one of the best program of ophthalmology in the country of, in Iowa. Then she went, she took her board. She got number one score in the country in the board.

Then she went to retina, which is, like, a male specialty, I think-- about eight or nine women specialize in retina. And she's doing OK from-- from professional point of view.

There were some problem with life here. We live with my mother. And my mother, when she came here-- we used to live separate in the war when we, in Warsaw when we were married.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] it's not so easy after so many adult people in different situation. My mother was always a professional. In this country she was nobody. She couldn't-- of course, there was no way for her to go back to a job.

She knew some English. She learned some English, but it was very difficult life for her. She was practically limited to our apartment. We didn't have the time to travel or go over there. Each one was trying to get back to profession and take care of this.

My mother passed away. She had a few strokes, and she died in 1986. As I say, she was not very religious person, but she was, she knew about the holidays and the Jewish tradition.

And that was something of a surprise in her very small-- not will, but statement she left for us-- she requested that if she would die, she wants to be buried in a very much Jewish tradition-- want to be buried by [? Hebrew-- ?] you know, in-- she'd have [NON-ENGLISH].

So that's what's happened. And she's buried here in Adat Shalom Cemetery. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] That's basically what's going on. Right now, I'm a cardiologist. I finished my program. I work in private practice in a big medical group.

My wife is dermatologist, work in the same place. My daughter was out of state. Now, she's back here, but work in different places. I, for many, many years, I didn't practice violin, because I've figured I'm not going professional so I'm not going to do it.

And about, about a few years ago, I began to do it again. It was difficult, but I'm enjoying. So I'm, I'm enjoying now, so if I'm retired, I think that's what I'm going to do-- play violin, maybe, maybe playing with a computer and so on.

You ask if I'm going back to Poland? Yes, I'm been in Poland, matter of fact, a week ago. I got still quite a few friends in Poland. I-- many of my friends were in the railway station when I was leaving Warsaw and knew exactly what's going on.

So the whole issue of so-called Polish antisemitism to me, is very much-- how to put it-- very much not well recognized by many people in this country. That's a different story.

INT: And you still consider yourself Jewish?

EM: Yes, but I am considering myself Jewish in the same way as probably American Jews. It's a question of whether it's-- first, you're Jewish, or American, you're Polish. OK, I consider myself a Polish Jew who immigrated United States.

I'm extremely grateful to this country and the helping with very, very difficult situation. And whatever I expected-- with all of the trouble that got through here, but that was not related to anything to this country as general-- but that was normal everyday living and certain people who were behaving maybe not, like, the correct, but really, in the situation and where I was in very, very difficult situations-- were very, very helpful.

And some of Jewish organizations behaved the same way. For example, I have no words to describe how HIAS behaved well and Joint. And then when they helped-- I didn't expect anything, but they were able to give me whatever support that I needed.

And when they come to this country, no question was ever asked about my wife not being Jewish, never. When I came to this country I got a feeling one of the reason why I was, for example, placed in apartment in Ohio, because somebody who was probably well positioned had a-- need the coupons, you know, those apartments-- they couldn't do it better. Matter of fact, later on they changed it. And they, they, they, I think they improved this.

INT: Do you have anything else you'd like to add?

EM: Yes and no. Maybe I will say that I'm very grateful to Mr. Spielberg that he-- I think it's very noble of him that he undertook this, this, this project. And I hope that it's going be some way hopeful.

I realize that some people may say I was a small child, maybe I don't remember. By my friends I'm known that I got a pretty good memory. They call my brain always computer brain.

I, this thing which I remember, I remember quite well, unfortunately. We didn't talk about it for many, many years. And I'm not very often coming back to this. Right now, I feel that what's happened happened. That's my personal tragedy.

My-- for me, my Holocaust Memorial Center, unfortunately, it's not here for me. Every time I go to Warsaw, I go to my-- the Jewish cemetery. I like that my-- I got-- some calls it [? mourning grave, ?] my parents, and that's-- [SOBS] that's my Holocaust Memorial.

